.Dd November 26, 2026
.Dt GPU-CHECK 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm gpu-check
.Nd say whether a GPU of this machine works with our driver
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -load
.Op Fl -assume-loaded
.Op Fl -check Ar id , Ns Ar id
.Op Fl -list
.Op Fl -json | Fl -jsonl
.Op Fl -report Ar file
.Op Fl -registry Ar dir
.Op Fl -pciconf-file Ar file
.Op Fl -fake Ar file
.Op Fl -capture Ar dir
.Op Fl -device Ar selector
.Op Fl -module-dir Ar dir
.Op Fl -arch Ar arch
.Op Fl -db Ar path
.Op Fl -no-db
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility reads the PCI display devices of this machine, asks every
registered provider whether it covers each of them, and reports the
decision with the reason.
It never loads anything unless
.Fl -load
is given.
.Pp
The checks are registered in a library, one per check, and each one says
what it needs: the root of the machine, the driver loaded, or the checks
that must run before it.
The command holds no list of its own: it asks the registry what to run.
Adding a check is adding a function in the library, and nothing else.
.Pp
Without
.Fl -load
the tool only looks.
It finds the display devices of
.Xr pciconf 8 ,
reads the identity of each one, and runs the device decision: which
provider claims the card, which generation the card is, which board
record covers it, and which backend of ours would drive it.
A card that is refused is never touched.
.Pp
With
.Fl -load
the tool loads the firmware and the driver of every card the decision
served, and then runs every check.
The load needs root, because it loads kernel modules.
.Pp
With
.Fl -assume-loaded
the tool runs every check and loads nothing: the firmware and the driver
are already loaded, and it says which of them are there and which are not.
This is the mode of a machine that loaded the driver itself, such as a
test boot that loads it and then watches what happens.
The tool never loads a module in this mode, so the loader of the machine
is left alone.
.Pp
With
.Fl -check
the tool runs the checks that were named and the checks they depend on,
and says for the others that they were not asked for.
.Pp
The decision comes from the provider records, which are data and not code:
one record per provider, under
.Pa data/providers
in the source tree.
Every provider names the vendor it claims, the generations it covers and
the backends it offers; the decision core of the tool names no vendor at all.
The vendor knowledge lives in the provider records and in the probe adapters,
one file per vendor, under
.Pa scripts/adapters
in the source tree.
A generation is the base of a card and a board is a decorator on top of it,
so a board record may add a feature or override one, but it never decides
whether a card is supported.
.Pp
The output is one line per card and one row per check.
Every check that was not run says so, and says why: a check is
.Dq pass ,
.Dq fail ,
.Dq not tested
or
.Dq not available ,
and the reason names the cause.
The checks are:
.Bl -tag -width "no-linux-layer"
.It detect
the card was found and its identity read: the vendor, the device id, the
subsystem, the generation and the board.
.It decide
the provider was chosen or refused, and why.
.It firmware
the firmware the card needs was loaded.
.It driver-loaded
the driver modules were loaded.
.It device-open
the device opened and its identity confirmed against the card that
.Xr pciconf 8
named.
.It identity
the identity of the card, as the resource manager sees it.
.It memory
memory was allocated, mapped, written and read back.
.It channel
a channel was created, released, measured and cleaned up.
.It uvm
the unified memory interface.
.It prime
the path that imports the display of another card.
.It display
the display and console path.
.It no-linux-layer
no module of the Linux layer is loaded.
.It native-modules
the modules of this card that are loaded are native.
.El
.Pp
The tool is written in Python 3 and uses nothing but its standard library,
so it needs
.Xr python3 8
on the machine.
.Pp
The output holds no personal data: no host name, no user name, no home
path and no serial.
The UUID of a card is hashed, never printed.
The
.Nm
sends nothing anywhere.
.Pp
Every run is recorded in a database, and the first word of the command
line may name a command that reads that record back instead of making a
run:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
gpu-check history [--last N]
gpu-check show RUN
gpu-check compare RUN RUN
gpu-check export RUN
gpu-check properties RUN [--changed] [--counters]
.Ed
.Pp
The commands are described in the section
.Sx READ-BACK COMMANDS .
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "--pciconf-file file"
.It Fl -load
Load the firmware and the driver of every card the decision served, and
run every check.
Needs root.
It is the only mode that changes the machine.
A load run reads the provider records of the installed tree only, and it
loads only the modules that are installed under
.Pa /boot/modules ,
as root-owned files.
.It Fl -assume-loaded
Run every check and load nothing: the firmware and the driver are already
loaded.
The tool says which of the modules of the card are loaded and which are
not, and it never calls
.Xr kldload 8 .
The checks that open the card need the root of the machine, and say so
when the run is not one.
This mode and
.Fl -load
cannot be given together.
.It Fl -check Ar id , Ns Ar id
Run the checks with these ids, and the checks they depend on.
The ids are the ones
.Fl -list
prints.
A check that is not registered is a usage error: the message names the id
that is not one and the checks that are.
Without
.Fl -load
a check that needs the driver says so instead of running.
.It Fl -list
List the checks and what each one needs.
Takes no other option.
.It Fl -json
Print one JSON document.
Its schema is
.Pa schema/gpu-check-1.schema.json .
.It Fl -jsonl
Print JSON Lines: a header record, one record per card, one record per
check and an end record.
Every line is flushed as it is written, so a reader sees the rows as they
come.
Its schema is
.Pa schema/gpu-check-stream-1.schema.json .
.It Fl -report Ar file
Write the JSON document to
.Ar file ,
to attach to a report.
Nothing is sent anywhere.
The document holds no name, no home path and no serial number.
.It Fl -registry Ar dir
Read the provider records from
.Ar dir
instead of the default: the
.Pa data/providers
directory of the source tree, or the installed one.
.It Fl -pciconf-file Ar file
Read the
.Xr pciconf 8
output from
.Ar file
instead of running
.Xr pciconf 8 .
For tests, and for a machine where
.Xr pciconf 8
is not installed.
.It Fl -fake Ar file
Answer the checks from
.Ar file
instead of from the machine.
A fake run loads nothing, needs no root, and says in its output that it
was a fake run.
The file is a JSON object with the keys
.Pa kldload ,
.Pa identity ,
.Pa device_open ,
.Pa memory ,
.Pa channel ,
.Pa uvm ,
.Pa prime ,
.Pa display
and
.Pa modules ;
every one of them is optional, and each names a status
.Pa pass ,
.Pa fail ,
.Pa not_tested
or
.Pa not_available
with a message.
The answer of a check that reads the card is an object:
.Pa identity
takes
.Pa status ,
.Pa pci_vendor ,
.Pa pci_device ,
.Pa uuid
and
.Pa message ,
and the other checks take
.Pa status
and
.Pa message .
.Pa kldload
is an object of module names, and every one of them takes
.Pa status
and
.Pa message ;
.Pa ok
is a synonym of
.Pa pass
for a module load.
The steps of a probe may be answered one by one, under
.Pa memory_steps
and
.Pa channel_steps ;
a step the file does not name passes.
A file that says nothing about a probe does not answer it: the probe was
not tested, and the row says so.
.Pa modules
is the list of the modules the machine of the fake run holds.
An answer may also be a map of card selectors to answers, so one file can
answer two cards of one machine apart.
A status that is not one of the four, or an answer without a status, is
refused: the file cannot be used, and the exit status is 2.
.It Fl -capture Ar dir
Replay a recorded run instead of looking at this machine.
The directory holds a
.Pa capture.json
file, of the schema
.Pa gpu-check-capture/1 ,
with the cards of that run, the modules it loaded, the identity of each
card and the steps of the probes.
A replay needs no root, loads nothing, and says in its output that it
replayed a recorded run.
.It Fl -device Ar selector
Check only the card with this selector, for example
.Pa pci0:0:1:0 .
A selector that matches no card is a usage error: the exit status is 2 and
the message names the selector and the cards of the machine.
.It Fl -module-dir Ar dir
Load the modules from
.Ar dir
as well as from the default one
.Pa /boot/modules .
A test boot keeps its modules somewhere else; the option may be given more
than once, and every directory must hold a root-owned file that nobody else
may write, or the module is not loaded.
.It Fl -arch Ar arch
Pretend the machine is
.Ar arch ,
for example
.Pa amd64
or
.Pa arm64 .
The name is written as the records write it, so a spelling of the same
architecture in upper case is accepted; a name that is not an architecture is
a usage error.
.It Fl -db Ar path
Write the record of the run to
.Ar path
instead of the default.
The default is
.Pa /var/db/gpu-check/
for root and
.Pa $XDG_STATE_HOME/gpu-check/
for everybody else.
The file is a SQLite database in the write-ahead log mode, so it may be
read while a run is writing to it.
The schema of the file is versioned, and a file whose version is newer than
the one this tool knows is refused: the refusal names both versions.
.It Fl -no-db
Keep no record of the run.
The run works exactly as it does with a record, and nothing is written.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print the usage and exit.
.El
.Sh READ-BACK COMMANDS
The commands below read the record of the runs back.
They need no root, they change nothing, and every one of them takes
.Fl -json
and
.Fl -jsonl .
.Bl -tag -width "compare RUN RUN"
.It Cm history Op Fl -last Ar n
The runs that are recorded, the newest first.
.Fl -last
limits the answer to the last
.Ar n
runs.
.It Cm show Ar run
One run: the cards of it and the rows of their checks.
.It Cm compare Ar run Ar run
Two runs, and what ended differently in them.
.It Cm export Ar run
The document the run printed when it was made.
With
.Fl -json
it is the same document, byte for byte, as the
.Fl -json
of the run itself.
.It Cm properties Ar run Op Fl -changed Op Fl -counters
The properties of a run, and the class of each of them.
.Fl -changed
shows only the properties that changed during the run.
A counter is left out unless
.Fl -counters
is given.
.El
.Pp
The documents answer against the schemas that are registered in
.Pa schema/samples.json :
.Pa schema/gpu-check-history-1.schema.json ,
.Pa schema/gpu-check-compare-1.schema.json
and
.Pa schema/gpu-check-properties-1.schema.json .
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "data/providers/nvidia-open.json"
.It Pa data/providers/nvidia-open.json
The provider record of the NVIDIA driver: the generations it covers, the
firmware each of them needs, the backends it offers and the policy floor.
The device id lists and the feature flags of the generations come from
NVIDIA's own
.Nm supported-gpus.json ,
which is under the zlib licence; the record names the file and its
checksum.
.It Pa schema/gpu-check-1.schema.json
The schema of the JSON document.
.It Pa schema/gpu-check-db-1.sql , Pa schema/gpu-check-db-2.sql
The migrations of the database, one file per version of the schema.
They are installed under
.Pa /usr/local/share/gpu-check/schema
and applied when the file is new and when the file is older than they are.
.It Pa scripts/adapters
The probe adapters of the source tree, one file per vendor, named after the
vendor id of the cards it opens.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 3n
.It 0
Every card a provider claims is served.
.It 1
A check failed.
A refused card is a refusal, not a failed check: the decision row of a
refused card does not count here.
.It 2
A usage error, or a provider registry that cannot be used.
A selector that matches no card is a usage error too.
.It 3
No card is claimed by any provider.
.It 4
The machine could not be looked at: the
.Xr pciconf 8
output could not be read.
.It 6
Some cards are served and some are refused.
.It 7
No claimed card is served.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Look at the cards of this machine and report the decision:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
gpu-check
.Ed
.Pp
Load the driver of every card the decision served and run the checks
(as root):
.Bd -literal -offset indent
doas gpu-check --load
.Ed
.Pp
Measure a card whose driver is already loaded, without touching the
loader:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
doas gpu-check --assume-loaded
.Ed
.Pp
Run one check and the checks it depends on:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
gpu-check --check memory
.Ed
.Pp
Write a report to attach:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
gpu-check --json --report gpu-check.json
.Ed
.Pp
Look at one card of a mixed machine:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
gpu-check --device pci0:0:2:0
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sqlite3 1 ,
.Xr gpu-check.db 5 ,
.Xr kldload 8 ,
.Xr kldstat 8 ,
.Xr pciconf 8 ,
.Pa docs/DEVICE-DECISION.md ,
.Pa docs/GPU-CHECK-DB.md
